  <h1>A Tale of Two Choices</h1>

  <h2>Style Based Implementation</h2>

  <p>The original Avisi plugin used a style to place the numbering in front of
the heading elements while editing content and rendered the numbers inline when
viewing the content.  In many respects this is an ideal choice.  In the editor,
the numbers are always viewable on heading elements but not editable.  Be
rendering inline the numbering was viewable in the browser or exported formats
such as pdf and word. But there are some downsides.  The new shared editor
introduced in Confluence 6 only propogates content changes and the style
changes applied by the numbered heading code do not get relayed to the other
sim editors. So when two or more people are editing a document, they will see
inconsistent numbering in their view.  The other difficulty is the server code
needs to have some means of identifying and rendering the numbering into the
html.  The most practical approach to that problem, and the one Avisi used, is
place all of the content into the body of a macro.  This is a functional but
less than ideal limitation of the approach.</p>

  <h2>Inline Content Based Implementation</h2>

   <p>The Keysight fork of the Avisi plugin took a different approach to the
problem.  Instead of applying a style to the heading in the editor, the number
was prepended to the heading.  Since content is being added, it is relayed to
the other shared editors.  Since the work is all performed in the editor, the
difficulty of having to identify and render the numbers on the server for
viewing goes away.  This makes it easy to implement a macro to trigger the
numbering algorithm for the body of the page rather than just inside the body
of a macro.  The downside with this approach is the user is given the ability
to edit the numbers.  The algorithm does a pretty good job at fixing them to
keep everything consistent, but it does provide a less than perfect user
experience.</p>

    <p>The primary motivation for developing the second approach used in the
Keysight fork was to reduce the long term risk to the content.  In the style
based approach all of the content is trapped inside the body of a macro.
Should the plugin ever become unsupported or the team desire to get rid of it,
they first need to find every instance of the macro and pull the content out.
In the second approach, instead of placing the content in the body of a
Numbered Heading macro, a Numbered Heading For Page macro is placed in the body
which activates the numbering algorithm.  Should that plugin be removed, a
warning will be displayed letting users know the macro can no longer be
rendered and the automatic numbering in the editor will stop - but all of the
content and the numbering from when the plugin was active remains visible and
editable.</p> 

  <h1>Transitioning Between the Approaches</h1>

  <p>The Avisi plugin provided two modules for the numbered heading macro.  An
XHTML version used by modern versions of Confluence and a wiki markup versions
for content writing in older versions.  The Keysight plugin provides three.
XHTML and wiki markup macros that implement the original Avisi style-based
approach and a third XHTML macro that implements the new inline content
approach.  By default, all three modules are disabled.  Administrators can
setup the plugin to work in one of three ways.  One approach is to not enable
any of the macros that number content in their body and push end users to the
Numbered Headings For Page macro.  This is the recommended approach for
Confluence instances that have not been using the Avisi plugin.  For sites that
have been using the Avisi plugin, the administrator can activate the legacy
modules which introduces a hybrid mode were old content will continue to
operate as is has before, but now authors can also use the <b>Numbered Headings
for Page</b> macro.  The last approach is to enable the
numberedheadings-transition module.  In this mode all of the content in
pre-existing Avisi macros will render without numbering until the page is
edited and resaved.</p>

   <p>To migrate away from the style based approach to the new inline content
approach, the recommendation is to enable the numberedheadings-transition macro
then manually updated every page with one or more instances of the macro.  To 
update a page, edit the page, insert a <b>Numbered Headings For Page</b> macro,
copy the contents of the <b>Numbered Headings</b> macro into the body of the 
page and finally delete the <b>Numbered Headings</b> macro.  Once all instances
of the <b>Numbered Headings</b> macro have been removed, it may be useful to
disable it.  This is not required as the macro provides the ability to 
apply a second numbering to a portion of the page.</p>

   <h2>Enabling modules to tune the mode of the plugin</h2>

   <h3>No Body Based Macros (default)</h3>

   <p>To block usage of the body based numbered heading macros, disable the
following modules: Javascript for the Legacy Numbered Headings Macro
(nh-tinymce-js-resources), numberedheadings (numberedheadings-transition),
numberedheadings (numberedheadings-legacy), numberedheadings
(numbered-headings-legacy-macro). This is the default configuration.</p>

   <h3>Hybrid Mode - old and new approaches available</h3>

   <p>To turn on the hybrid mode where existing instances of the <b>Numbered
Headings</b> macro will continue to operate as before but users can now use the
<b>Numbered Headings For Page</b> macro, activate the Javascript for the Legacy
Numbered Headings Macro (nh-tinymce-js-resources), the numberedheadings
(numberedheadings-legacy) and the numberedheadings
(numbered-headings-legacy-macro) plugins while leaving the numberedheadings
(numberedheadings-transition) disabled.</p>

   <h3>Inline Conent Only Mode with Body Based Macros</h3>

   <p>To enable the body based macros with the new inline content approach
macro, activate the numberedheadings (numberedheadings-transition) module and
deactive the the Javascript for the Legacy Numbered Headings Macro
(nh-tinymce-js-resources), the numberedheadings (numberedheadings-legacy) and
the numberedheadings (numbered=headings-legacy-macro)</p>

